mplementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage consistency:\n39\t`triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 7-epic output is expected and not\n40\tpenalized.\n41\t\n42\tThe decomposition fails on one structural defect: a data/artifact dependency\n43\tbetween E003 and E004 on the supported configuration matrix that is discoverable\n44\tfrom epic content but absent from the DAG. All other dimensions are clean.\n45\t\n46\t## Criterion Details\n47\t\n48\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n49\tEvery HLR maps to at least one epic: HLR-1→E001, HLR-2→E002, HLR-3→E003,\n50\tHLR-4→E004+E005, HLR-5→E006, HLR-6→E007. All P0 and P1 HLRs are covered; the\n51\tP2 HLR-6 is covered rather than deferred. Priority inheritance is correct:\n52\tE001 (P0) gates only P0 work (E002, E003). E007 is docs-authoring and its P0\n53\tcorrectly derives from the strategy's `priority: Critical` via the\n54\tdocs-authoring exception, not inheritance — explicitly documented in the\n55\tsummary, so its P1/P2 dependencies (E004/E005/E006) are not violations. No epic\n56\tmaps to HLRs at multiple priority levels, so no priority-collapse or bundling\n57\tanalysis is required; the HLR-4 split across E004/E005 is a component/team\n58\tboundary split, not a priority split, and is justified in the traceability\n59\tmatrix.\n60\t\n61\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n62\tThe diagram (`graph TD`, dependency → dependent) matches frontmatter\n63\t`dependencies` exactly on all 8 edges; no cycles; no invalid references; no\n64\tbranch files exist. Rule 11 docs-last coverage is complete (E007 blocked by all\n65\tfive implementation epics). The E001→E002/E003 gates are true gates with\n66\t`rewrite` semantics and populated `gate_failure_impact`; the summary explicitly\n67\tjustifies the absence of E001→E004/E005/E006 edges (HLR-4/HLR-5 are fully\n68\tspecified and unaffected by the assessment outcome). The E004→E005 edge is a\n69\tjustified Rule 12 artifact-producer→consumer edge (validation builds against\n70\tthe promoted integration). No konflux/repo/license epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10\n71\tparallelism does not apply.\n72\t\n73\tHowever, the cross-check for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies finds one:\n74\t**E003 and E004 share the supported configuration matrix with no edge between\n75\tthem and no coordination note.** E003 owns defining and validating the matrix\n76\t(HLR-3), with the AC \"every configuration it declares as supported has passed a\n77\tverification run with recorded evidence\" and \"no configuration is listed as\n78\tsupported without verification evidence.\" E004's scope explicitly records the\n79\tTrustyAI guardrails integration's \"supported state in the configuration\n80\tmatrix\" — the same artifact. Since the two epics run in parallel, either E003's\n81\tmatrix omits the guardrails configuration (an incomplete GA matrix for an\n82\tintegration HLR-4 requires to be supported at GA), or E003 must verify a\n83\tconfiguration whose supported state E004 has not yet landed, or the matrix\n84\tentry and its evidence land outside E003 with no DAG relationship — none of\n85\twhich the DAG or its justification resolves. This is a missing edge where a\n86\tdata/artifact dependency exists (critical), so the criterion scores 0.\n87\t\n88\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n89\tComponent/team tuples are consistent and separate: E001-E004 (Llama Stack /\n90\tRHOAI GenAI), E005 (TrustyAI / TrustyAI), E006 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard),\n91\tE007 (Documentation / RHOAI GenAI, docs-authoring). E002 vs E003 is a justified\n92\twork-product split on the same component/team (API surface work vs\n93\tpolicy/matrix deliverables, distinct review cycles). E004 vs E005 correctly\n94\tsplits HLR-4 across the team boundary. No epic is clearly oversized relative\n95\tto one logical change. Two minor notes (neither reduces the score):\n96\t\n97\t- E002/E003 sizing is an estimate until the assessment lands; the summary\n98\t  manages this via the gate but records no concrete re-split trigger.\n99\t- E004/E005 each carry user-facing documentation bullets while E007's scope\n100\t  also includes a guardrails-integration documentation section; E007 is\n101\t  correctly derived (Rule 11, \"consistent with the validated integration\n102\t  behavior\"), but the venue split between the implementation epics' docs and\n103\t  the Red Hat docs content process is not stated.\n104\t\n105\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n106\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the definitive GA scope) changes\n107\twhat E002/E003 contain, and both carry `gated_by: E001` with `action: rewrite`\n108\tand populated `fallback_approach`. E001 appears in both gated epics' direct\n109\t`dependencies` lists. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts\n110\t(assessment-governed API surface work, posture documents and validated matrix,\n111\tpromoted integration, validation report, default-on flip, published docs). No\n112\tepic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`; no\n113\tnon-investigation `gated_by` is set.\n114\t\n115\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n116\tSignal fields match epic types: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal\n117\trubric) with `ai_signals: null`; E002-E007 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric)\n118\twith `investigation_signals: null`. All seven rationale files exist and their\n119\tvalues match the frontmatter. Calibration against `.context` verifies the\n120\tarchitecture claims: llama-stack.md confirms Tech Preview maturity,\n121\tOpenAI-compatible REST APIs, LlamaStackDistribution CRD, vLLM/Milvus provider\n122\tmodel; trustyai.md confirms Stable (GA), guardrails, and TrustyAIService\n123\tinstances per project; dashboard.md confirms per-component feature flags via\n124\tthe OdhDashboardConfig CR — so `architecture_claims: 1` with cited rationales\n125\tis correct on every implementation epic, including E007's honest note that the\n126\t`Documentation` component has no architecture-context file. The E006\n127\t`open_questions: 1` is consistent with the summary's argument that the\n128\tstrategy's single open question (GA scope) does not affect HLR-5; E002/E003\n129\t`open_questions: -1` correctly reflects their assessment gates.\n130\t\n131\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n132\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are\n133\tpresent where applicable: E004 has a disable/rollback AC (replacement of an\n134\texperimental integration), E006 has an administrator opt-out AC verified by\n135\ttest (default-flip reversibility), E007 has technical review sign-off recorded\n136\t(docs-authoring). No konflux chain exists, so the build-pipeline AC does not\n137\tapply. No AC states an externally-controlled precondition — the support-organization\n138\tsign-off and review-time risks are correctly placed in Risks/Notes (E003) and\n139\tthe summary's Health Warnings. No epic body references sibling epics by draft\n140\tID; upstream work is referred to by name (\"the GA-readiness assessment\", \"the\n141\timplementations\").\n142\t\n143\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n144\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: the gap inventory\n145\tincl. scattered TP support-ticket feedback (E001), API stability with the\n146\tlast-two-TP-releases upgrade path (E002), support posture (E003), guardrails\n147\tpromotion and its cross-team validation (E004/E005), Dashboard default-on with\n148\tentitlement and opt-out behavior (E006), and GA docs uplift with TP migration\n149\tnotes (E007). Out-of-scope boundaries (no new feature development, no\n150\tdeprecating alternative serving paths) are respected. The strategy's open\n151\tquestion is carried forward as the E001 gate rather than silently dropped. The\n152\tstrategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section (recorded as a Health\n153\tWarning), and the docs-authoring obligation arises from HLR-6 — E007 satisfies\n154\tRule 11. No conditional branches are declared or needed: the single unknown is\n155\tunbounded (an assessment outcome), correctly handled as a gating Investigation\n156\trather than bounded branch files.\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0fccc655639e079","agent_id":"a6f5ddbc43ece28a3","session_id":"e26389fa-88dc-429a-9f52-aa484b1f6aa7","uuid":"4c8beab7-58da-4f65-b4c6-3ce13abcfb3d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:55:16.905Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-923fd48e65646733","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
