{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ea7491356b227e0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability (RHAISTRAT-9014)\n10\t\n11\tTriage: neither below-threshold nor docs-only. The strategy spans three\n12\tcomponents (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) owned by three teams, carries\n13\tP0/P1/P2 HLRs, and contains a genuine unknown (the GA scope itself is not yet\n14\tinventoried — the strategy's open question states the size of the stability\n15\tand hardening work is an estimate until HLR-1's assessment lands). Full\n16\tmulti-epic decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | API stability guarantees: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, TP upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported status in Llama Stack distributions | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | TrustyAI-side validation of the guardrails integration for GA distributions | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Enable Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | GA documentation uplift with TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n29\t\n30\tPriorities: E001 is P0 because it transitively enables all P0 GA work\n31\t(HLR-2, HLR-3). E007's P0 derives from the docs-authoring priority exception\n32\t(strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not from inheritance — it\n33\tdoes not push priority upstream.\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t  E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability\"]\n40\t  E001 --> E003[\"E003 Support posture\"]\n41\t  E004[\"E004 TrustyAI integration (distro)\"] --> E005[\"E005 TrustyAI validation\"]\n42\t  E002 --> E007[\"E007 GA docs\"]\n43\t  E003 --> E007\n44\t  E004 --> E007\n45\t  E005 --> E007\n46\t  E006[\"E006 Dashboard default-on\"] --> E007\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\t## DAG Justification\n50\t\n51\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n52\t|------|------|-----------|\n53\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The GA-readiness assessment produces the definitive GA scope; the size and content of the API stability and hardening work is explicitly an estimate until it lands (strategy open question). True gate: `rewrite` — the assessment outcome can change what the stability work includes. |\n54\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Same assessment defines the GA bar for support posture (which configurations must be supported, what the error-handling/scale gaps are). True gate: `rewrite`. |\n55\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 12 (artifact producer → consumer) | The TrustyAI team's validation epic verifies the integration wired into the GA distribution; the validation deliverable builds against the integration artifact, so it is serial. |\n56\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Migration notes and GA docs describe the versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade path once built. |\n57\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation must reflect the published SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. |\n58\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs cover the TrustyAI guardrails integration as supported. |\n59\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs reflect the TrustyAI-side supported-integration validation outcomes. |\n60\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe default enablement for entitled clusters and the opt-out. |\n61\t\n62\tNo edge E001 → E004 / E005 / E006: the guardrails integration promotion, its\n63\tvalidation, and the Dashboard default-on are fully specified by their HLRs\n64\t(HLR-4, HLR-5) — the assessment inventories API stability, upgrade paths,\n65\tscale limits, and error handling, which map to HLR-2/HLR-3 scope, and does not\n66\tchange whether these epics exist or what they deliver. They start in parallel\n67\twith the assessment.\n68\t\n69\tNo konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics: Llama\n70\tStack, TrustyAI, and the Dashboard are all existing platform components\n71\t(architecture context files present, Konflux onboarded per context files), so\n72\tRule 20 onboarding chains do not apply.\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n75\t\n76\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n77\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n78\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | The assessment itself is the epic deliverable. |\n79\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | API versioning, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n80\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E003 | SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix — a distinct logical change (policy/matrix definition and validation) from E002 (API surface work), so two epics for the same component/team per the work-product boundary rule. |\n81\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E004, E005 | One HLR covered by two epics across the component/team boundary: the distribution-side promotion (Llama Stack / RHOAI GenAI) and the service-side validation (TrustyAI / TrustyAI team). TrustyAI is otherwise passive in this strategy (Stable GA component; no other code changes), and the different-team validation triggers a separate validation epic rather than an AC on the nearest active component's epic. |\n82\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E006 | Dashboard default enablement for entitled clusters. |\n83\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E007 | Docs-authoring epic; priority P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy `priority: Critical`), not from HLR-6's P2 label — the P2 reads as relative value. |\n84\t\n85\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n86\tNo epic maps to HLRs at multiple priority levels, so no priority-split or\n87\tbundling analysis is required. HLR-2 and HLR-3 are both P0 and are deliberately\n88\tseparate epics (distinct deliverables and review cycles), not a priority split.\n89\t\n90\t## Health Warnings\n91\t\n92\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n93\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n94\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were inferred from the\n95\t  architecture context files (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard) and ACs\n96\t  were derived from the HLRs. Proceeding per Step 1's guidance.\n97\t- **No Staff Engineer Input section** — no overrides to log.\n98\t- **Docs priority inversion (managed)**: HLR-6 is P2 but the docs-authoring\n99\t  epic is P0 by the deterministic exception rule (strategy priority Critical).\n100\t  Reviewers should read E007's P0 as scheduling-neutral; per the rules it does\n101\t  not propagate upstream.\n102\t- **Scope sizing unknown (managed)**: E002/E003 scope is an estimate until the\n103\t  assessment lands; both carry `gated_by` with `rewrite` semantics and fallback\n104\t  approaches, and the assessment epic is first on the critical path so the\n105\t  estimate resolves before the work starts.\n106\t- **Component mapping note**: E007 uses canonical component `Documentation`\n107\t  (present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`) for the docs-authoring epic; the\n108\t  content concerns Llama Stack GA. There is no architecture-context file for\n109\t  the `Documentation` component itself — expected for a docs-process label,\n110\t  not a new-c