inations.\n50\t- Record validation evidence: guardrails policies enforced through the\n51\t  integration, service behavior under distribution load and error conditions\n52\t  relevant to the GA bar.\n53\t- Make any TrustyAI-side adjustments required for the integration to be\n54\t  supported (service behavior, documentation of the integration contract from\n55\t  the TrustyAI side), if validation surfaces them.\n56\t- Document TrustyAI-side integration requirements (provisioning, sizing,\n57\t  supported TrustyAIService settings) for customers using the integration —\n58\t  as the TrustyAI-side engineering-source reference; the user-facing\n59\t  documentation is authored separately through the Red Hat docs content\n60\t  process.\n61\t\n62\t## Acceptance Criteria\n63\t\n64\t- A validation report exists covering the guardrails integration scenarios\n65\t  exercised jointly with the distribution-side integration, with\n66\t  pass/fail results recorded.\n67\t- Supported version combinations (TrustyAI service × Llama Stack\n68\t  distribution) are documented, and each declared combination has validation\n69\t  evidence.\n70\t- Guardrails enforcement through the integration is verified: policies\n71\t  configured on the TrustyAI service produce the expected outcomes for\n72\t  requests routed from a Llama Stack distribution.\n73\t- TrustyAI-side integration documentation exists, covering provisioning and\n74\t  supported settings for the integration.\n75\t- Any TrustyAI-side issues found during validation are either fixed or\n76\t  recorded with disposition (supported workaround or documented limitation).\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability\n79\t\n80\t- **HLR-4 (P1)**: Safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n81\t  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions — the\n82\t  TrustyAI-owned half of this requirement (service-side validation and\n83\t  support of the integration).\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b03c5b8988ba3b1","ag